# Build output
build/
app/build/
.gradle/

# Local SDK paths and machine-specific settings — never committed; each checkout
# generates its own, and a stale one breaks builds on other machines.
local.properties

# Android Studio / IntelliJ
.idea/
*.iml
.cxx/
captures/

# Keystores and signing material. The release key must never enter version control:
# anyone holding it can publish an update that Android accepts as genuinely yours.
*.jks
*.keystore
keystore.properties

# Internal handoff notes. These track what has and hasn't been run on real hardware, what is
# still unproven, and what was tried and abandoned — useful while working, not what the project
# should say for itself in public.
# No Markdown is published except the README. Notes and handoffs describe intent and
# in-progress state, and intent drifts from the code faster than anyone updates it — a stale
# document that contradicts the source is worse than no document. They stay on disk.
#
# README.md is the exception because it is not a working note: GitHub renders it as the
# repository's front page, so dropping it would leave the project with no description at all.
*.md
!README.md

# OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
# GNOME's default screenshot filename. These get dropped here while checking a UI change
# against a device or a mockup and are never worth keeping once the change lands.
Screenshot From *.png

# Scratch space for things generated while working on the app rather than shipped in it —
# the launcher-icon preview page is rendered from the res/ drawables on demand, so committing
# it would just be a second copy of the icon that can silently fall out of date with the
# drawables it was rendered from.
.cache/

# Unrelated to this app — a Linux filesystem-hardening script and its output, which happen to
# live in this directory. The report enumerates weak/world-writable paths on the machine that
# generated it, so it is specifically the sort of thing not to publish. Both stay on disk;
# they are simply not this project's to track.
harden-fs.sh
hardening-report.html

# Release build outputs. Same reasoning as build/ above: these are generated from the
# tracked sources, and a 9 MB APK committed per release would live in every future clone.
# Ship built APKs as GitHub Release assets attached to the version tag instead.
app/release/

# Kotlin compiler daemon session files — regenerated on every build.
.kotlin/

# Icon source art dropped in the repo root while redrawing the launcher icon. The shipped icon
# is the set of density buckets under app/src/main/res/drawable-*dpi/, generated from this file;
# tracking the original as well would put a second, diverging copy of the same artwork in the
# tree. Keep it on disk for the next regeneration, out of the repository.
newicon.png
